#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221087 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "e9855de8-af52-5bc0-aae2-1dbc5906a659" :question/math-academy-id "e-21179" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}t^{3} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}t^{3} & t^{3} - 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= t^{3} \\cdot 4 - (t^{3} - 5) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} - 2t^{3} + 10 \\\\\n&= 2t^{3} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221088 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef2af54-fc18-5908-9adb-fb3e2d92c098" :question/math-academy-id "e-21180" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4e^{2t} \\\\ e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R$." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(x_{1}, x_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}-4e^{2t} & e^{t} \\\\ e^{2t} & 2e^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (-4e^{2t}) \\cdot (2e^{t}) - e^{t} \\cdot (e^{2t}) \\\\\n&=-8e^{3t} - e^{3t} \\\\\n&=-9e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221089 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions to Systems of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "79080694-1e56-57e5-8249-9aadbcea4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21181" :question/problem "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-4x_{1}(t) - 2x_{2}(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n2. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n3. The function $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nis defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}x_{11}(t) & x_{12}(t) \\\\ x_{21}(t) & x_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t^{2} + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (t^{2} + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2t^{2} - 4t + 2 \\\\ -4t^{2} + 8t - 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot$\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(2t + 1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t + 1 \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot (2t + 1) + 1 \\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot (2t + 1) + (-2) \\cdot (-4t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1 - 2t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1 - 2t\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot t + 1 \\cdot (1 - 2t) \\\\ -4 \\cdot t + (-2) \\cdot (1 - 2t)\\end{bmatrix}; \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}✓$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$. Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(b, c) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2t + 1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1 - 2t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (2t + 1) \\cdot (1 - 2t) - t \\cdot (-4t) \\\\\n&= (2t + 1)(1 - 2t) + 4t^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R$. Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\big\\{\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4d9d68-59b6-5209-8265-e1e062c422bf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "5e593968-af4d-5666-af75-3cb617187b66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220060}] :question/id #uuid "c55f9ab8-e7b6-5ec8-9696-dd445096cc3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-322731" :question/problem "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t)\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin (3t) \\\\ \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"identifying-linearly-independent-vector-valued-functions\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions to Systems of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0211048-1573-5a10-9aca-ccfb8d67c24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220062} {:answer/id #uuid "2a830313-9639-587a-b48c-d45715662c58" :answer/